<div class="article-body">

<p>I put a jar of mung bean sprouts and a tray of radish microgreens side by side in my kitchen. My girlfriend asked why I was growing the same thing twice. She was wrong, and most people make the same mistake. Sprouts and microgreens look like tiny twins. They are not related. One grows in 3 days in a jar of water. The other takes 2 weeks in soil under a light. One is food-safety rated high risk. The other has no major outbreak on record.</p>

<p>Here's the short version: <strong>sprouts are a faster, cheaper, riskier option. Microgreens are slower, safer, and more nutrient-dense.</strong> I grow both, and I'll tell you exactly when each one wins and which I'd start with if you're new.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I'm not a nutritionist. I'm just someone who grew a lot of seeds indoors and read a lot of safety sheets so you don't have to. If I can sort this out in my apartment kitchen, you can too.</p>

<p>Here is the thing that surprised me most: <strong>the food safety gap is way bigger than the nutrition gap.</strong> I expected the difference to be about vitamins. Turns out the real difference is about what's safe to eat raw. More on that in a minute.</p>

<h2 id="not-same">1. They Are Not the Same Thing At All</h2>

<p>Sprouts are germinated seeds grown in water only. You soak them, then rinse and drain them 2 to 3 times a day. In 2 to 5 days, you eat the whole tiny plant: the seed, the root, and the shoot. Nothing gets trimmed. No light needed, no soil needed, just a jar and a sprouting lid.</p>

<p>Microgreens are baby vegetable plants grown in soil, coco coir, or a reusable mat for 7 to 21 days under a grow light. You harvest only the stem and leaves, cutting above the soil line. The roots stay in the tray. Popular kinds are radish, sunflower, broccoli, kale, and pea shoots, and there are close to a hundred varieties.</p>

