<h2 id="nutrition">2. Nutrition: Which One Is Actually Denser</h2>

<p>Sprouts have their own strengths. Because you eat the whole seed, you get more bioavailable protein, digestive enzymes, and fiber than you do from just the shoot. Broccoli sprouts are famous for sulforaphane, the compound researchers keep studying for its health perks. If your goal is protein and enzymes, sprouts deliver it cheap and fast.</p>

<p>Microgreens win on vitamins and antioxidants. A University of Maryland and USDA study found microgreens can hold 4 to 40 times more vitamins and antioxidants than their mature vegetable versions. Red cabbage microgreens tested high in vitamins C, E, and K. Because they grow under light, they make chlorophyll and carotenoids that sprouts never produce.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: <strong>4 to 40 times.</strong> That's the vitamin and antioxidant gap between microgreens and the full-grown vegetable. Sprouts are healthy. Microgreens are a concentration of the plant's nutritional peak, packed into one tiny cut.</p>

<p>So on pure nutrient density, microgreens win on vitamins, sprouts win on protein and fiber. No single winner for everyone. But there is a category where the winner is not even close.</p>

<h2 id="safety">3. The Safety Gap Nobody Talks About</h2>

<p>Here is where it gets interesting. I assumed both were equally safe to eat raw. I was wrong.</p>

<p>Sprouts grow in warm, dark, humid water. That is exactly the environment where Salmonella, E. coli, and Listeria multiply fastest. And because you eat the whole sprout, including the seed and root that sat in water, any bacteria on the seed has a direct path to your plate. The FDA officially classifies raw sprouts as a high-risk food and advises children, the elderly, pregnant women, and anyone with a weak immune system to avoid them entirely.</p>

<p>Microgreens are different. They grow in open air with light and airflow, conditions that don't favor bacterial explosions. You cut only the stem and leaves above the soil. The roots and seed hulls stay in the tray. There have been a few microgreen recalls over the years, but no major foodborne illness outbreak has been directly tied to microgreens in the US. Sprouts have a documented outbreak history going back decades.</p>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. Then I read the FDA's own guidance and realized the "easy" option was the risky one. I thought sprouts were the no-brainer. Turns out they're the one that needs care: clean hands, clean jar, clean water, every single rinse.</p>

<h2 id="effort">4. Setup, Time, and Shelf Life Compared</h2>

<p>Hands down, sprouts are faster and cheaper to start. You need a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=sprouting+jar+wide+mouth+mason&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">wide mouth sprouting jar</a> (about $10), some <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mung+bean+sprouts&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mung bean seeds</a> ($8 a bag), and water. First harvest in 3 days. No light, no soil, no electricity. Three bucks of seeds can keep you in fresh crunch all month.</p>

<p>But sprouts have a shelf life problem. They last a few days, maybe a week in the fridge. You're locked into a rinse-and-drain cycle 2 to 3 times a day, forever, or you waste the batch. Forgot to rinse for a day? They can smell funky and go slimy. I killed more sprout jars than I care to count.</p>

<p>Microgreens are slower to start but easier to run. One <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=1020+microgreen+seed+tray&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1020 seed tray</a> and some <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=broccoli+microgreens+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">broccoli microgreen seeds</a> get you a full tray in about 2 weeks. You water them, they sit under a cheap light strip for 16 hours, and you forget about them. A tray lasts 7 to 14 days in the fridge after harvest. Same daily routine as a houseplant, way less babysitting than a sprout jar.</p>

<p>The catch is the setup. Microgreens want <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=LED+grow+light+strip+microgreens&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">a grow light strip</a>, trays, and a growing medium, and they eat up shelf space for 2 weeks at a time. My first tray cost about $35 all in. Sprouts cost me $18. That's the trade: sprouts win on speed and price, microgreens win on staying power and nutrients.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">5. Which Should You Grow?</h2>

<p>Different tools, different jobs. Here is how I actually decide now.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Short on money, short on space, want crunch this week?</strong> Grow sprouts. A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=sprouting+jar+wide+mouth+mason&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">sprouting jar</a> and some seeds get you going for under $20. Just respect the rinse schedule and keep everything clean.</li>
  <li><strong>Want the most nutrients per bite and don't mind waiting 2 weeks?</strong> Grow microgreens. Higher vitamin density, safer raw, lasts twice as long in the fridge. Broccoli and radish are the easy starters.</li>
  <li><strong>Pregnant, elderly, or feeding someone with a weak immune system?</strong> Pick microgreens. Raw sprouts are the one the FDA tells these groups to skip entirely.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is the honest truth: microgreens are the "better" option on paper, more nutrients and safer raw. But they are also more gear, more time, and more space than the hype implies. If you want results in 3 days with no equipment beyond a jar, sprouts are honestly the better starter. The risk is real but manageable if you keep your jar and hands clean. I grow both. Sprouts for fast crunch, microgreens for flavor and feeding the salad bowl longer.</p>

<p>One honest thing about microgreens: the first tray costs more than you expect, about $35, and you'll burn half of week one just figuring out water so the seeds don't rot. My first tray was patchy. My second tray filled the whole 10x20 surface. Tray two is where it clicks. If yours is ugly on the first try, that's normal, not a failure.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> They are not the same food, no matter how many grocers bury them in the same display. Sprouts = fast, cheap, needs a strict rinse routine. Microgreens = slow, safer, denser, and easier to babysit once running. Start with whichever matches your week. If you want the safest nutrient punch with the least daily fuss, set up a microgreen tray. Check current prices below. My 1020 trays run about $8 for a 10-pack and last for years.</p>

